  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Realize later",
   "texto": [
    "Para decir que, después de hacer algo, descubres algo, se usa **-고 보니(까)**: 먹다 → **먹고 보니** (al comerlo, resultó…), 읽다 → **읽고 보니** (al leerlo, resultó…), 사다 → **사고 보니** (al comprarlo, resultó…).",
    "이 책을 **읽고 보니** 정말 재미있었어요 = al leerlo, resultó ser muy divertido."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "With surprise",
   "texto": [
    "The second part almost always expresses what you discovered or a surprise.",
    "In the purchase of this, it was very expensive."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Fingir",
   "texto": [
    "**-는 척하다** is added to the present modifier of a verb to fake an action, and **-(으)ㄴ 척하다** to the modifier of an adjective. **N인 척하다** is used with nouns. In the past, he uses the last modifier: **자는 척했어요** = faked sleep; **아픈 척했어요** = faked sick; **학생인 척했어요** = faked being a student. The form alone does not mean that the speaker creates the lie."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Surprise reaction",
   "texto": [
    "**-다니(요)** expresses surprise, unbelief, or reaction to information, usually repeating part of what was said: **벌써 끝났다니요!** = What do you mean it's over?! It is not the same as **-다고요**, which repeats or confirms an affirmation with emphasis."
   ]
  },
